      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 American Academy of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Mosby, Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Migration of airway smooth muscle cells (ASMCs) might contribute to increased airway smooth muscle mass in asthma. T(H)17 cells and T(H)17-associated cytokines are involved in the pathogenesis of asthma and might also contribute to airway remodeling."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "We sought to explore the possibility that migration of ASMCs might contribute to airway remodeling through the action of T(H)17-related cytokines."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The effect of exogenous T(H)17 cytokines on ex vivo human ASMC migration was investigated by using a chemotaxis assay. The involvement of signaling pathways, including p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK), extracellular signal-regulated kinase 1/2 MAPK, nuclear factor κB, and phosphoinositide 3-kinase, was also examined."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "We demonstrated that IL-17A, IL-17F, and IL-22 promote migration in a dose-dependent manner. We further demonstrated that ASMCs express receptors for IL-17RA, IL-17RC, and IL-22R1. Using mAbs directed against these receptors, we confirmed that T(H)17-associated cytokine-induced migration was dependent on selective receptor activation. Moreover, IL-17A and IL-17F exert their effects through signaling pathways that are distinct from those used by IL-22. The p38 MAPK inhibitor BIRB0796 inhibited the migration induced by IL-17A and IL-17F. PS1145, an inhibitor of nuclear factor κB, abolished the IL-22-induced migration."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "These data raise the possibility that T(H)17-associated cytokines promote human ASMC migration in vivo and suggest an important new mechanism for the promotion of airway remodeling in asthma."
          }
        ]
      },
